/*
 * The manifest, as resource 1 (CREATEPROCESS_MANIFEST_RESOURCE_ID) of type 24
 * (RT_MANIFEST) -- the ids Windows looks for when deciding whether to elevate.
 * Linked in rather than shipped beside the exe so a copied or renamed setup
 * cannot lose it.
 *
 * Why setup.manifest carries no comments of its own, and must not:
 *
 *   XML forbids "--" anywhere inside a comment body. The first version of that
 *   file explained itself in prose full of em-dash pairs, which is invalid XML;
 *   Windows would not parse it, and refused to start the program at all with
 *   "the application has failed to start because its side-by-side
 *   configuration is incorrect" -- a message that says nothing about which of
 *   the many things it covers went wrong. The manifest is now data only and
 *   the reasoning lives here, where C comments have no such rule.
 *
 * What it asks for:
 *
 *   requireAdministrator. Everything setup does needs it: creating the driver
 *   service, bcdedit, writing under Program Files, and the scheduled task that
 *   starts the guest at logon. Without a manifest, NT 6 and later start it
 *   unelevated even for an administrator account, because a double-click is
 *   not an elevated launch, so it would get most of the way in and fail at the
 *   service.
 *
 * What it deliberately omits:
 *
 *   Any <compatibility> / <supportedOS> block. GetVersionEx reports 6.2 on
 *   Windows 8.1 and later unless an executable claims support for the newer
 *   versions, and setup.c depends on that being harmless: host_is_nt6() only
 *   asks whether this is NT 6 or later, and everything that lies about being
 *   6.2 has the three properties it cares about, which are UAC, kernel-mode
 *   code signing and Task Scheduler 2.0. Declaring supportedOS would make
 *   GetVersionEx tell the truth, change no answer, and invalidate the
 *   reasoning written beside that function.
 */
1 24 "setup.manifest"
